  "title": "King on generalized staircases with equal odd blocks",
  "batch_label": "primary",
  "kind": "resolution-paper",
  "original_status": "proved",
  "scope": "For every odd positive integer r and every positive integer k, let D_k be the nonempty partition consisting of r copies of kr, then r copies of (k-1)r, and so on down to r copies of r. A legal King move deletes its first row, its first column, or both, by exactly one unit, and is allowed only when the resulting partition is nonempty. Let G be its normal-play Sprague-Grundy value. If q is the unique integer with 0<=q<r+2 and q congruent to k modulo r+2, then G(D_k)=0 when q is odd, G(D_k)=1 when q is even and nonzero, and G(D_k)=2 when q=0. This is exactly the universal Conjecture 5.1 frozen in source.md, without restrictions to finite parameters or changes to empty-position conventions.",
  "gate_basis": "Current released terminal, manifest, PDF, TeX freeze, snapshot files, provenance and four audits agree.",
